<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enrico Dandolo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1107–<a href="/years/1205">1205</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enrico Dandolo was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a> from 1192, elected in old age and nearly blind. He led the Venetian part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, diverting it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which the crusaders sacked in 1204. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> gained a large share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, the basis of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Mar</a>, and he died in Constantinople in 1205.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo.jpg" alt="Enrico Dandolo" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grosso_of_Enrico_Dandolo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Enrico Dandolo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> around 1107, a member of the prominent Dandolo family and the son of the jurist Vitale Dandolo. His uncle, also named Enrico Dandolo, was patriarch of Grado. Little is known of him before his father's death in 1174, since Vitale lived into his nineties and his sons were not emancipated until then. His first important political role came in the crisis of 1171 and 1172 between Venice and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. He made trips to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as a ducal legate in 1183 and 1184, and in 1184 the emperor Andronicus I agreed to release imprisoned Venetians and restore their quarter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Dandolo became doge on 1 June 1192, after Orio Mastropiero abdicated, as the second doge chosen by a council of forty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. He was then old and blind. The commonly given birth year rests on Marino Sanuto the Younger, writing three centuries later, who stated that Dandolo was eighty-five at his election. How he lost his sight is not known: the Chronicle of Novgorod says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> blinded him, while Thomas F. Madden argues that a blow to the head between 1174 and 1176 caused cortical blindness. In 1194 he reformed the Venetian coinage, introducing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> grosso, the first nearly pure silver, high-denomination coin minted in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in over five centuries.</p><p>In 1202 envoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for a fleet. The agreement set four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks for each knight and horse, with Venetians promising fifty armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> in return for half the spoils. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> could not pay the full 85,000 marks and still owed 34,000, so Dandolo proposed that they repay it from the spoils and help Venice retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>. Zara fell on 24 November 1202, and the Venetians were excommunicated, which Dandolo kept secret. The Crusaders then agreed to restore Alexius Angelus to the Byzantine throne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was conquered and sacked on 12 April 1204. Dandolo declined the imperial crown, accepted the title of despot, and Venice received three-eighths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in the Partitio Romaniae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dandolo died in May or June 1205, reportedly from an infection caused by a hernia, and was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>. Accounts differ on what became of his tomb: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> are said to have thrown his remains into the Bosporus in 1261, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> to have had Venetian tombs broken after 1453. His acquisitions lasted only until the collapse of the Latin Empire in 1261. His granddaughter Anna Dandolo married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned">Stefan Nemanjić</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1217, making him an ancestor of much of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enrico Dandolo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Fourth Crusade the Venetians transported the crusading army, diverted it to Zara in 1202 and to Constantinople, which fell in 1204; Doge <b>Enrico Dandolo</b> secured Venice a claim to three-eighths of the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Crusaders sacked the city in 1204 it was a Latin Catholic cathedral until 1261, and the doge <b>Enrico Dandolo</b> was buried inside.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1207 Marco Sanudo, nephew of the doge <b>Enrico Dandolo</b>, led a private Venetian expedition that conquered Naxos and other Cycladic islands, sharing some with fellow adventurers.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-archipelago">Duchy of the Archipelago</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1170" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Enrico Dandolo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1170">What was here in 1170? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am a man old and feeble, who should have need of rest, and I am sick in body; but I see that no one could command and lead you like myself, who am your lord.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in St Mark's, Venice, 1202 (Villehardouin, The Conquest of Constantinople) &#183; translator not named &#183; Villehardouin's account; he was there; the doge was blind and very old &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6032/pg6032-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If you will consent that I take the sign of the cross to guard and direct you, and that my son remain in my place to guard the land, then shall I go to live or die with you and with the pilgrims.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in St Mark's, Venice, 1202 (Villehardouin, The Conquest of Constantinople) &#183; translator not named &#183; Villehardouin's account; he was there &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6032/pg6032-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
